---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Protein Powder Rankings",
    tags: [
      "aldi vs costco protein powder",
      "elevation vs kirkland protein powder",
      "aldi or costco protein powder",
      "kirkland vs elevation whey"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Aldi Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-aldi-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Head to Head"} badge="Research-Backed" author={author} dates={dates} label="Our standards">
      <Fragment slot="title">Aldi vs Costco Protein Powder</Fragment>
      Elevation by Millville and Kirkland Signature are the two cheapest store-brand wheys most American shoppers can buy. Here is how they compare on the label, on cost per 25 g of protein, and on everything the price tag leaves out.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#at-a-glance">At a Glance</a>
      <a href="#labels">The Two Labels</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#size-and-access">Size and Membership</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing and Transparency</a>
      <a href="#taste-and-use">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#verdict">Which to Buy</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          If you shop at both stores, the short answer is that Costco's Kirkland Signature Whey is the leaner, isolate-led powder with the bigger bag, and Aldi's Elevation whey is the sweeter, heavier 30 g shake you can buy in a small tub without a membership. On cost per 25 g of protein they land closer than most people expect, and which one wins depends on the tub size on your Aldi shelf and the price at your Costco warehouse that week. This guide puts the two labels side by side, shows the break-even maths so you can settle it with the prices in front of you, and explains when the cheaper powder is not the better buy.
        </p>

        <div id="at-a-glance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Aldi vs Costco at a Glance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both stores sell one own-brand whey and put their weight behind it. At Aldi it is Elevation by Millville, a whey concentrate and isolate blend sold in chocolate and vanilla. At Costco it is Kirkland Signature Whey Protein, which arrived in 2024 as a 5.4 lb bag and launched in creamy chocolate. Neither store names the manufacturer, neither powder carries a sport certification we could find, and both are backed by a generous return policy. Beyond that, they are built for quite different shoppers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Aldi's range is narrow by design. Elevation whey is effectively the only protein powder in the core range, and stock varies by store. Costco's range is narrow too, but less so: a typical warehouse carries three to six powders at a time, usually Kirkland and Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard alongside a plant option from Orgain and a rotating name brand such as Ascent, Levels, Isopure or Dymatize. So the real comparison is Elevation against Kirkland, with Costco's name brands as a fallback that Aldi does not offer.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Aldi Elevation whey</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Costco Kirkland whey</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein / serving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g (label)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 g (label)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories / serving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 170-180</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 130</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sugar / serving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein source</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey concentrate and isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Isolate-led blend with concentrate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pack size</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 lb tub, or 24 oz in newer listings</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5.4 lb bag, about 70 servings</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Membership needed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sport certification</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None found</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None found</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The figures above come from the labels as reported at the time of writing, and they vary by flavor and formula version. Two numbers jump out. Elevation gives 5 g more protein per serving, but it does so with a much larger scoop and roughly 40 to 50 more calories. Kirkland is the leaner powder per gram of protein, which matters if you are counting calories rather than just hitting a protein target. The serving sizes are not equivalent, so comparing scoop for scoop will mislead you.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you only want the headline: Kirkland is the better powder on paper and usually the cheaper protein if you already pay for a Costco membership. Elevation is the better choice if you do not have a membership, want a smaller commitment than a 5.4 lb bag, or prefer a sweeter, thicker shake. The rest of this guide explains where those generalisations break down. Our full <a href="/best-aldi-protein-powder/">best Aldi protein powder</a> guide covers Elevation in more depth, and our <a href="/best-costco-protein-powder/">best Costco protein powder</a> rankings cover the whole warehouse range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="labels" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Two Labels, Side by Side
          </h2>

          <p>
            Per the labels reported by reviewers and nutrition databases, Elevation whey gives 30 g of protein per serving at roughly 170 to 180 calories, with about 2 g of sugar. The serving is large, reported at 44 to 49 g of powder, and some tubs describe it as two scoops. That means roughly 61 to 68 percent of the powder by weight is protein. The remainder is carbohydrate, flavoring, sweeteners and thickeners, which is part of why the shake tastes rich and why it carries more calories than a leaner whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kirkland Signature Whey gives about 25 g of protein per scoop at roughly 130 calories, with about 4 g of carbohydrate and 1 g of sugar, per the label at the time of writing. The blend is led by whey protein isolate, with concentrate after it. A leading isolate usually means less lactose and less fat per gram of protein, and it is the main reason the calorie count is lower. On protein per calorie, Kirkland comes out ahead: roughly 19 g of protein per 100 calories against roughly 17 g for Elevation.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sweeteners and extras</h3>

          <p>
            Elevation is sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium and includes natural and artificial flavors, sunflower lecithin and cellulose gum, per the ingredient list on Open Food Facts. Older tubs advertised added amino acids and creatine, and reviewers report the formula later dropped that blend. If your tub lists added amino acids, check whether they count toward the protein figure. For more detail on what is in the current tub, see our sibling guide, <a href="/is-elevation-protein-powder-good/">is Elevation protein powder good</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kirkland is a simpler pitch: a mainstream sweetened whey with normal macros, within a few grams and a few calories of Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard, which has been the reference whey for decades. Neither powder is a clean-label product, and neither is trying to be. If you want to avoid artificial sweeteners entirely, both are the wrong buy, and an unflavored whey that you sweeten yourself is the better route.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Compare protein per calorie, not protein per scoop.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Elevation's 30 g looks bigger than Kirkland's 25 g, but it comes in a scoop with about 40 to 50 more calories. If you are cutting, Kirkland gives you more protein for the same calorie budget; if you just need a big dose in one shake, Elevation's larger serving is convenient.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Allergen labelling deserves a careful read on both. Whey is dairy, so milk is a given. One database entry for Elevation also lists wheat and egg, likely as a shared-facility statement, while Aldi markets the current tubs as gluten free. Check the actual tub or bag in your hands rather than the website if you have a wheat, egg or celiac concern, because store-brand formulas and suppliers can change without much announcement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Cost per 25 g: The Break-Even Maths
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sticker price is the least useful number on a protein powder. The one that settles a comparison is cost per 25 g of protein: multiply grams of protein per serving by servings per container, divide the price by that total, and multiply by 25. That puts a small Aldi tub of 30 g servings and a large Costco bag of 25 g scoops on equal terms. Our <a href="/tools/protein-cost-calculator/">protein cost calculator</a> does the arithmetic for you if you would rather not.
          </p>

          <p>
            On the Aldi side, the 2 lb Elevation tub at about $20 holds roughly 18 to 21 servings depending on which label and year you read, or about 540 to 630 g of protein. That works out at around $0.80 to $0.95 per 25 g. The newer 24 oz tub at $19.79 holds roughly 14 servings if the scoop size is unchanged, which lands around $1.10 to $1.20 per 25 g. Those prices come from aldi.us and coverage we found in early November 2026, and shelf prices can differ from the website.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kirkland's price varies by warehouse and moves with member deals, so rather than quote a figure that may be stale, here is the break-even. A 5.4 lb bag of about 70 servings at 25 g each holds roughly 1,750 g of protein, which is 70 portions of 25 g. So the bag's price divided by 70 is its cost per 25 g. To beat the 2 lb Elevation tub at $0.80 to $0.95, the bag needs to cost less than about $56 to $66. To beat the 24 oz tub at $1.10 to $1.20, it needs to cost less than about $77 to $84.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">If you compare against</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Elevation cost per 25 g</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Kirkland bag wins below</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Elevation 2 lb tub, about $20</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.80-0.95</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $56-66 per bag</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Elevation 24 oz tub, $19.79</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.10-1.20 (est.)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $77-84 per bag</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Our Costco guide places Kirkland at the cheapest end of the warehouse's wheys on protein per dollar at typical member prices, and in the budget tier for the market as a whole. Read the shelf tag at your warehouse, divide by 70, and you have your answer in ten seconds. If the result is close, the deciding factors are the ones in the next sections: whether you want 5.4 lb of one flavor, whether you already pay for a membership, and how leaner protein per calorie fits your goals.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not forget sales. Costco runs instant savings on protein powder several times a year, and a discounted Gold Standard or Ascent tub can briefly undercut Kirkland itself. Aldi rarely discounts Elevation, so its price is steady rather than spectacular. That asymmetry means the cheapest choice can flip from month to month. Our sibling guide, <a href="/how-much-is-aldi-protein-powder/">how much is Aldi protein powder</a>, tracks Elevation's pricing and compares it with food sources of protein as well as rival powders.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="size-and-access" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Tub Size, Membership and Getting Hold of It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pack size is the biggest practical difference between the two. Kirkland comes in a 5.4 lb bag of about 70 servings. At one shake a day, that is more than two months of the same flavor, and at the time of writing Kirkland launched in chocolate only. If you discover you dislike it after a week, you are looking at a lot of powder, although Costco's return policy takes much of the sting out. Elevation's 2 lb or 24 oz tub is a far smaller commitment, lasting two to three weeks at one serving a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Membership is the second difference. You cannot buy Kirkland whey in a Costco warehouse without a membership, and the basic Gold Star membership was $65 a year when we checked in early November 2026. If you already have one for groceries or fuel, that cost is sunk and Kirkland's per-gram price is the real price. If you would be joining just for protein powder, the fee wipes out the savings for a long time. Aldi has no membership, so Elevation's shelf price is the whole cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Availability cuts both ways. Aldi runs on a small, rotating range, and plenty of shoppers report going weeks without seeing Elevation on the shelf; new flavors and the plant powder have behaved more like Aldi Finds that arrive and sell out. Costco stock also varies by warehouse and region, and some items appear only on Costco.com, where prices differ from the warehouse. The practical difference is that when Kirkland is out, Costco usually has Gold Standard on the same pallet row, while an Aldi with no Elevation has nothing else to offer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Online, neither store brand is easy to buy at a fair price. Elevation is not sold by Aldi on Amazon, and third-party resellers sometimes list tubs at a markup that erases the point of buying it. Costco.com sells some powders to members for delivery, with an online price that can be higher than the warehouse. If neither store has stock, a name brand ordered online will usually beat a resold store-brand tub, and our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> rankings show which ones come closest on price per gram.
          </p>

          <p>
            Storage is a minor point, but it matters if you buy big. An unopened whey tub or bag keeps for many months stored cool and dry, but a 5.4 lb bag that is opened and resealed daily for two months needs a decent seal to stay clump-free. If you live alone or drink a shake only a few times a week, check the best-by date and do the maths on how many weeks a bag will actually last. Our sibling guide on <a href="/how-many-servings-in-aldi-protein-powder/">how many servings are in Aldi protein powder</a> works through that for the Elevation tub sizes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Testing, Transparency and Who Makes Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            Neither store names its manufacturer. Aldi does not say who makes Elevation, and Costco does not say who makes Kirkland whey, and we could not find a reliable source that names either. That is normal for store brands, and it does not mean anything is wrong. It does mean you are trusting the retailer's supplier standards and quality control rather than a brand with a public testing record you can look up.
          </p>

          <p>
            We found no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing for either powder at the time of writing. For most gym-goers and people adding protein to breakfast, that does not change much. For athletes in drug-tested sports, it is decisive: those certifications screen lots for banned substances and confirm that the label matches the contents, and a positive test is the athlete's responsibility regardless of what the tub said. If you compete, neither store brand is the right buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here Costco has a clear advantage that Aldi cannot match. Ascent whey, sold in Costco warehouses since mid-2025 per the brand's announcement, is Informed Sport certified, and Optimum Nutrition promotes Informed Choice on many Gold Standard flavors. So a Costco member who needs a certification can stay in the same store and pay modestly more. An Aldi shopper who needs one has to go elsewhere. Our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">best NSF certified protein powder</a> guide covers the certified options sold online.
          </p>

          <p>
            Independent heavy-metal results are thin for both. We are not aware of published lab results for Elevation or Kirkland, which is not evidence of a problem but is not reassurance either. Whey generally tests lower for heavy metals than plant powders and chocolate-heavy formulas, so a plain whey blend is a reasonable starting point. If contaminant testing matters to you, our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> lists brands that publish their results.
          </p>

          <p>
            Health caveat, briefly: protein powder is food, not medicine, and this comparison is not medical advice. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant, have a milk allergy or take medication that affects your appetite or digestion, talk to a doctor or registered dietitian about your protein target before adding a daily shake from either store. Both powders are dairy-based, so neither suits anyone with a milk allergy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste-and-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Taste, Mixing and Everyday Use
          </h2>

          <p>
            We have not run a side-by-side taste test, so what follows is buyer feedback rather than our own verdict. Elevation reviews generally rate the taste as decent for the price rather than exceptional, with chocolate the more commonly reviewed of its two flavors. The large serving and added thickeners tend to give a fuller, sweeter shake, which suits people who drink it as a snack or a dessert substitute. Its sweetness is the most common complaint from people who prefer a lighter flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kirkland's buyer feedback has been broadly positive on taste and mixing, with some people finding it a little thin in water. That is consistent with an isolate-led powder in a smaller scoop: less carbohydrate and thickener means a lighter body. A common fix is to mix it with milk, which adds roughly 8 g of protein per cup and a creamier texture, or to blend it with a banana and ice. Because it launched in chocolate only, flavor variety is limited compared with name brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            For cooking, the differences matter more than in a shaker. A leaner, isolate-led whey such as Kirkland tends to stir into Greek yogurt and overnight oats without adding much bulk, but it can turn rubbery if baked on its own. Elevation's larger, sweeter serving can make baked goods and oats noticeably sweeter, so you may want to cut other sugar from the recipe. In both cases, mix the powder with a little liquid first and stir out the lumps before adding it to the rest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lactose is worth a thought if regular whey bothers you. Isolate carries less lactose than concentrate, and Kirkland's blend leads with isolate, so it may sit better than Elevation for mildly sensitive drinkers, although a blend never gives the full benefit of a pure isolate. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> guide explains the difference. If both cause trouble, Costco's Dymatize ISO100 or Isopure, when stocked, are low-lactose isolates; Aldi has no equivalent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about how you will actually use it. If one shake after the gym is the plan, either powder works and the cost-per-gram and convenience factors should decide it. If you drink protein two or three times a day, the leaner Kirkland scoop keeps calories in check and the large bag means fewer trips. If you only use powder occasionally in oats or smoothies, a smaller Elevation tub is less likely to go stale before you finish it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which One Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you already have a Costco membership and tolerate dairy, buy Kirkland Signature Whey. It is the leaner powder, it is isolate-led, it comes in a bag big enough to last two months, and at typical member prices it sits at the cheapest end of the warehouse range. Check the shelf price against the break-even figures above. If Kirkland is out of stock, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard is usually right next to it and costs only slightly more per gram.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do not have a Costco membership, buy Elevation at Aldi. Paying $65 a year to save a few cents per shake does not add up unless you would use the membership anyway. Look for the 2 lb tub, which is among the cheapest flavored wheys you can buy at around $0.80 to $0.95 per 25 g. The 24 oz tub is still fair value, just not a standout, and it is a sensible size if you want to try the flavor before committing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The exceptions</h3>

          <p>
            If you compete in a tested sport, skip both store brands and buy a certified powder. Costco makes that easy with Ascent's Informed Sport certification; at Aldi you will need to shop elsewhere. If you are counting calories closely, Kirkland's 130-calorie, 25 g scoop beats Elevation's 170 to 180 calories for 30 g. If you want a thicker, sweeter shake that feels like a treat, Elevation suits you better, and buyer reviews back that up.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you avoid dairy, neither store-brand whey is an option. Costco usually stocks Orgain plant protein, at about 21 g per two-scoop serving, while Aldi's Elevation plant powder has been patchy and was out of stock when we checked. If you shop at both, the cheapest strategy is simple: buy Kirkland when the bag price lands below the break-even, buy Elevation when you run out between Costco trips, and always compare cost per 25 g rather than the sticker.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing scoop for scoop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Elevation's 30 g serving and Kirkland's 25 g scoop are different sizes with different calorie counts. Comparing one scoop with another makes Elevation look like more protein for the money. Work out cost per 25 g of protein and protein per 100 calories, and the comparison becomes fair.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Joining Costco just for protein powder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A $65 Gold Star membership wipes out the per-gram savings on Kirkland for a long time if protein powder is the only reason you join. The bag is only cheaper in practice if you already hold a membership for groceries, fuel or other bulk shopping you would do anyway.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Elevation tub size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Elevation has been sold in a 2 lb tub and, in newer listings, a 24 oz tub at almost the same price. That changes its cost per 25 g by about a third, which can flip the comparison with Kirkland. Check the net weight on the label before deciding which store wins.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying 5.4 lb of an untried flavor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Kirkland's bag is about 70 servings, launched in chocolate only. If you have never tasted it, that is a long commitment. Costco's return policy helps, but a better plan is to try a friend's scoop or start with milk and a blender so a thin texture does not put you off.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a store brand is sport certified</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Neither Elevation nor Kirkland carried an NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing that we could find. Athletes in drug-tested sports should buy a certified powder, such as Ascent at Costco, and verify the exact product and flavor in the certifier's own database.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Aldi or Costco protein powder cheaper?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It depends on the tub and the warehouse price. The 2 lb Elevation tub at about $20 works out at roughly $0.80 to $0.95 per 25 g of protein. A 5.4 lb Kirkland bag of about 70 servings beats that if it costs less than roughly $56 to $66. Against the 24 oz Elevation tub, Kirkland wins below about $77 to $84.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which has more protein, Elevation or Kirkland?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Elevation lists 30 g of protein per serving and Kirkland about 25 g per scoop, per the labels at the time of writing. But Elevation's serving is much larger, at roughly 170 to 180 calories against about 130 for Kirkland. Per calorie, Kirkland delivers more protein, so it is the better fit if you are cutting or counting calories.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Kirkland protein powder better than Aldi's?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  On paper, Kirkland is the leaner powder, with an isolate-led blend, about 1 g of sugar and fewer calories per gram of protein. Elevation is sweeter and heavier, with a bigger 30 g serving. Neither carries a sport certification we could find. For most members Kirkland is the better buy; without a membership, Elevation is.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Do I need a Costco membership to buy Kirkland whey?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  To buy it in a warehouse, yes. The basic Gold Star membership was $65 a year when we checked in early November 2026. Costco.com also sells to members, sometimes at a different price from the warehouse. Third-party resellers may list Kirkland whey online without a membership, but usually at a markup that removes the savings.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Who makes Aldi and Costco protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Neither store names the manufacturer, and we could not find a reliable source that does for either Elevation or Kirkland. That is normal for store brands. It means you rely on the retailer's supplier standards and return policy rather than a brand's public testing record, which is fine for most people but not for tested athletes.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which is better for lactose sensitivity, Aldi or Costco?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Kirkland's blend leads with whey isolate, which carries less lactose than concentrate, so it may sit better than Elevation for mildly sensitive people. Costco also sometimes stocks low-lactose isolates such as Dymatize ISO100 and Isopure. Aldi has no isolate option. Anyone with a milk allergy should avoid both store-brand wheys entirely.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I return protein powder at Aldi or Costco?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Both stores have long offered generous return policies. Aldi's guarantee lets you return a product you are unhappy with for a refund or replacement, and Costco takes back most products it sells. That lowers the risk of trying either store brand, but check your store's current terms before you open a bag you are unsure about.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

